Ladybird now paints a non-repeating linear gradient whose stops are
outside 0%-100% by running its gradient line from its first stop to its
last, does the same with a conic gradient’s sweep, and collapses a
radial gradient’s negative stops to 0 with an edge color interpolated in
its interpolation space, scaling its ending shape for stops past 1.
Blink does the same in CSSGradientValue::AddStops(): It moves the
endpoints of a linear gradient and the angles of a conic one by
AdjustedGradientDomainForOffsetRange(), and for a radial one runs
ClampNegativeOffsets() and then AdjustGradientRadiiForOffsetRange().
WebKit’s normalizeStopsAndEndpointsOutsideRange() (StyleGradient.cpp)
rescales a linear gradient’s stops and endpoints, and for radial and
conic gradients collapses negative stops to 0 with a color from
interpolateColors() in the gradient’s interpolation method. Gecko’s
nsCSSGradientRenderer adjusts a linear gradient’s line and runs
ClampColorStops(), and moves a radial gradient’s negative stops to 0
with the interpolated color. Against Chrome 158 (engine-compare),
Ladybird now matches within one level per channel in the issue’s cases,
an angled gradient, a hard stop, oklab, oklch and hsl interpolation
(longer hue included), conic gradients with stops past both ends, and a
repeating gradient. Firefox 151 agrees, except in the oklab and hsl
cases, where its own colors differ from Chrome’s.
